uch of an inconvenience i'm used to  it by now so i don't really see the  issue  when it comes to treatments i i don't  i'm glad to have you weigh in on  particular treatments if you feel so  inclined and as though you can do so but  more generally speaking  what is the reason why some of these  widely available cheap drugs  enter the news cycle as possibly  efficacious treatments is it simply that  there's stuff lying around that's cheap  and doctors decide to to throw it at the  virus and see what happens  yeah i think that's part of it there are  i mean the the  the tendency is to take a drug that's  already approved say by the fda  unlicensed and you can use it off label  in people without too much trouble so  for a few drugs early on in the pandemic  some labs did experiments showing that  the drugs could inhibit virus  multiplication in cells and that was  immediately translated to usage in  people without a good clinical trial and  so none of those repurposing  have really resulted in anything  worthwhile  it's unfortunate because you would love  to have a pill that you could take at  the moment of your positive that would  help you a lot we still don't have that  and if you want to know why  besides these repurposings failing it's  because the billions of dollars were put  into vaccines rather than drugs and  that's that's the bottom line if we put  20 billion dollars into drugs we'd have  some right now is that right you're  confident that this is not an unsolvable  problem it's just not been we haven't  done put enough into it  absolutely and in fact i think we could  have had a drug before the pandemic even  started we could have had one ready and  it would have stopped the pandemic and  that's a failing of research and big  pharma for sure what type would it be an  antiviral or what type of drug would it  be it would be an antiviral because we  knew after sars1 that there were virus  similar viruses and bats that could  infect human cells and all we need